---
import ToolPage from '../../layouts/ToolPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is a ferritin of 25 low?',
    a: 'It is inside most laboratory reference ranges, which often start as low as 12, but below the level many clinicians treat as adequate. A cutoff of 30 ng/mL is about 92% sensitive and 98% specific for iron deficiency, so a ferritin of 25 alongside fatigue, hair shedding or breathlessness is worth discussing rather than dismissing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between normal and optimal ferritin?',
    a: 'Normal is a statistical description of the population the laboratory sampled — a range that includes people with undiagnosed iron deficiency. Optimal is a clinical judgement about the level at which most people feel and function well, usually placed around 30 to 100 ng/mL. The gap between the two is where a great many symptomatic people sit while being told their result is fine.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is ng/mL the same as µg/L for ferritin?',
    a: 'Yes, exactly the same. One nanogram per millilitre equals one microgram per litre, so a ferritin of 30 ng/mL and 30 µg/L are identical values. Unlike glucose or cholesterol, ferritin needs no unit conversion — only the label changes between laboratories.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can ferritin be normal and iron still be low?',
    a: 'Yes. Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant, meaning inflammation, infection and liver disease push it up independently of iron stores. Someone with both inflammation and iron deficiency can show a deceptively normal ferritin, which is why a full iron panel with transferrin saturation and CRP is the more complete picture.',
  },
];

const related = [
  { href: '/blog/ferritin-levels-chart/', text: "Ferritin levels chart: what's normal vs what's optimal" },
  { href: '/blog/low-ferritin-guide/', text: 'What does low ferritin actually mean?' },
  { href: '/blog/full-iron-panel/', text: 'The full iron panel: beyond ferritin' },
  { href: '/blog/iron-deficiency-vs-low-ferritin/', text: 'Iron deficiency vs low ferritin vs anemia' },
];

const refs = [
  { text: 'Short MW, Domagalski JE. Iron Deficiency Anemia: Evaluation and Management. American Family Physician.', href: 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2013/0115/p98.html' },
  { text: 'World Health Organization. WHO guideline on use of ferritin concentrations to assess iron status. NCBI.', href: 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK569877/' },
  { text: 'National Institutes of Health, Office of Dietary Supplements. Iron — Health Professional Fact Sheet.', href: 'https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/' },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Ferritin Level Checker: Normal vs Optimal"
  description="Enter your ferritin and see where it sits between the lab's normal range and the level clinicians call optimal. The gap between the two is where symptoms hide."
  short="Ferritin checker"
  faq={faq}
  related={related}
  refs={refs}
>
  <div slot="calculator" class="panel">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="val">Your ferritin (ng/mL or µg/L — same thing)</label>
        <input id="val" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="e.g. 25" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="sex">Sex</label>
        <select id="sex">
          <option value="f">Female</option>
          <option value="m">Male</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="out" id="out" role="status" aria-live="polite">
      <span class="big" id="big">—</span>
      <span class="note" id="note">Enter your ferritin to see where it falls.</span>
    </div>
    <p class="hint">Ferritin rises with inflammation, so a normal value doesn't fully rule out low iron. A full iron panel with transferrin saturation and CRP gives the complete picture.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why one number gets two different answers</h2>
  <p>
    A ferritin of 25 is the clearest example in all of blood work of the difference between
    <em>normal</em> and <em>adequate</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Laboratory reference ranges commonly start at 12 or 15 ng/mL. Those floors come from the
    statistical distribution of whoever the lab sampled — a population that included plenty of people
    with undiagnosed iron deficiency. Being inside the range means you are not in the bottom few
    percent of that group. It does not mean you have enough iron.
  </p>
  <p>
    Against that, a cutoff of <strong>30 ng/mL</strong> is roughly 92% sensitive and 98% specific for
    iron deficiency. The World Health Organization reserves 15 ng/mL for outright depletion. So there
    is a wide band — roughly 12 to 30 — where the report says normal and the clinical literature says
    deficient.
  </p>

  <h2>The bands this tool uses</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Ferritin (ng/mL)</th><th>Status</th><th>What it usually means</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 15</td><td>Depleted</td><td>WHO threshold for outright iron depletion</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15 – 30</td><td>Functionally low</td><td>Often labelled normal; symptoms are common here</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30 – 100</td><td>Optimal</td><td>The comfortable range for most adults</td></tr>
      <tr><td>100 – 200</td><td>Normal-high</td><td>Fine; worth noting if trending upward</td></tr>
      <tr><td>200 – 300</td><td>High</td><td>May reflect inflammation, liver issues or iron loading</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Above 300</td><td>Elevated</td><td>Warrants investigation</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The catch that cuts the other way</h2>
  <p>
    Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant: inflammation, infection, liver disease and obesity all raise
    it regardless of how much iron you have stored. That means a normal ferritin can mask a real
    deficiency when inflammation is present — the two effects cancel out on the report.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why ferritin alone is an incomplete test. Transferrin saturation stays low in deficiency
    even when inflammation lifts ferritin, and CRP tells you whether inflammation is in play at all.
    The <a href="/blog/full-iron-panel/">full iron panel</a> covers both.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do next</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Compare against your own history</strong>, not just the range. A slide from 70 to 40 to 25 is the finding, even though every one of those was reported as normal.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for the full panel</strong> — serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation, plus CRP to check for inflammation.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't self-supplement on a single reading.</strong> Iron overload is a real risk and the treatment differs by cause.</li>
    <li><strong>Bring symptoms with you.</strong> Fatigue, hair shedding, breathlessness on stairs and restless legs are the ones most often dismissed at a borderline ferritin.</li>
    <li><strong>Talk to a clinician</strong> if you are below 30 with symptoms, or above 300 at all.</li>
  </ol>
</ToolPage>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    const val = document.getElementById('val');
    const sex = document.getElementById('sex');
    const out = document.getElementById('out');
    const big = document.getElementById('big');
    const note = document.getElementById('note');

    function assess(v) {
      if (v < 15) return ['Depleted', 'Below the WHO threshold for iron depletion. Some laboratory ranges start lower still, which is how a result like this ends up reported as normal.', 'warn'];
      if (v < 30) return ['Functionally low', 'Most labs report this as normal, but 30 ng/mL is about 92% sensitive and 98% specific for iron deficiency — so this band is where symptoms commonly sit while the report says fine.', 'warn'];
      if (v < 100) return ['Optimal', 'This is the range most clinicians are comfortable with for adults.', 'ok'];
      if (v < 200) return ['Normal-high', 'No concern on its own. Worth noting if it has been climbing across tests.', 'ok'];
      if (v <= 300) return ['High', 'May reflect inflammation, liver issues or iron loading rather than good iron status.', 'warn'];
      return ['Elevated', 'Above the usual reference ceiling — this warrants investigation, including inflammation and iron overload.', 'warn'];
    }

    function run() {
      out.classList.remove('ok', 'warn');
      const v = parseFloat(val.value);
      if (!val.value || isNaN(v) || v < 0) {
        big.textContent = '—';
        note.textContent = 'Enter your ferritin to see where it falls.';
        return;
      }

      const [label, explain, cls] = assess(v);
      const labFloor = sex.value === 'm' ? 24 : 11;
      // The note earns its place only in the 15-30 band, where "normal on the report" and
      // "deficient in the literature" genuinely collide. Below 15 the band text already says it.
      const showNote = v >= 15 && v < 30 && v >= labFloor;

      big.textContent = v + ' ng/mL — ' + label;
      note.textContent = explain +
        (showNote
          ? ' A typical lab range for your sex starts around ' + labFloor + ', so this value is printed as normal.'
          : '');
      out.classList.add(cls);
    }

    [val, sex].forEach((el) => {
      el.addEventListener('input', run);
      el.addEventListener('change', run);
    });
  })();
</script>
